From 89ef9e4c6e0b1bff703a10eba9cdd562bccabae3 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yibo Wang <wyibo6109@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 14 Oct 2026 11:40:39 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] soc: qcom: oplus-camres: fd-driven state machine,
 exact-boundary child hooks

The ownership model is rebuilt around one principle: the kernel never
binds itself to userspace liveness.

States: UNCONFIGURED (boot - AP holds the bus, camera works with zero
userspace), CONFIGURING (a DEINIT is in flight, acquires queue),
CONFIGURED (INIT succeeded - per-use REVOKE/RELEASE arbitration), ERR
(the DEINIT owner died before INIT - acquires fail until a fresh
DEINIT/INIT cycle recovers).  Transitions are driven by /dev/camres
file objects and ioctls only:

  DEINIT   blocks until every AP user is done (explicit votes in
           ->consumers, device-link users in runtime PM usage), hands
           the bus to the SEE and unsubscribes AON; the calling fd
           becomes the owner
  INIT     owner-only, carries the userspace SEE-INIT ack result:
           0 = CONFIGURED, non-0 = back to UNCONFIGURED (vendor
           tolerance)
  ACQUIRE/RELEASE  per-fd votes, released automatically on fd teardown

Consumer drivers are no longer modified at all.  Children below the
adapter get a PM_RUNTIME device link (probe-direct power-on outside
runtime PM is covered by votes taken at the exact bus-notifier
boundaries: BIND_DRIVER acquires inline on the probing thread,
BOUND_DRIVER / DRIVER_NOT_BOUND release; a child with no driver never
votes).  The public acquire/release API stays exported for exotic
drivers.  sysfs is reduced to ap_hold (0600, DAC only - node
permissions are the single authority, no capability checks).

All ioctls require FMODE_WRITE; the node is created root:root and
chmod/chown is entirely a userspace matter.
